 ## Overview
-Topological Sort for Cell Projection (MPVO) is an $O(n)$ algorithm for computing a back-to-front visibility ordering of convex polyhedral cells in a convex grid [S1][S2, book][S3, unofficial]. It solves the occlusion ordering problem required for correct alpha-blending in object-order volume rendering by constructing a directed graph of pairwise occlusion relations between adjacent cells and performing a topological sort [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which may use simpler centroid sorts, MPVO guarantees a correct visibility order for convex meshes by respecting the transitive occlusion chain along viewing rays [S1][S3, unofficial]. It fails on non-convex meshes because it ignores occlusion across empty space between non-adjacent cells [S1][S3, unofficial].
+Topological Sort for Cell Projection (MPVO) is an $O(n)$ algorithm for computing a back-to-front visibility ordering of convex polyhedral cells in a convex grid [S1][S2, book][S3, unofficial]. It solves the occlusion ordering problem required for correct alpha-blending in object-order volume rendering by constructing a directed graph of pairwise occlusion relations between adjacent cells and performing a topological sort [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Tetrahedral Volume Rendering](820-tetrahedral-volume-rendering.md) or [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) which may rely on simpler centroid or distance-based heuristics, MPVO guarantees a correct visibility order for convex meshes by respecting the transitive occlusion chain along viewing rays [S1][S3, unofficial]. It fails on non-convex meshes because it ignores occlusion across empty space between non-adjacent cells [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A convex grid of convex polyhedral cells; a viewpoint $V$ (for perspective) or a projection direction vector $\vec{d}$ (for orthogonal).
-**Outputs:** A list of cells sorted in back-to-front order.
